import type { Question, FurtherReading } from "@/lib/types"

type SeedQuestion = Omit<Question, "id" | "created_at">

const SUMMARIES: Record<string, string> = {
  "Fundamentals of Security": "The CIA triad — Confidentiality, Integrity, and Availability — forms the foundation of information security. Non-repudiation ensures that a party cannot deny having performed an action, while Authentication, Authorization, and Accounting (AAA) control and audit access to resources. Security controls are categorised by type (preventive, detective, corrective, deterrent, compensating, directive) and by category (technical, managerial, operational, physical). Zero Trust is a security model that assumes no user or device should be trusted by default, requiring continuous verification. Gap analysis identifies the difference between an organisation's current security posture and its desired state.",
  "Cryptographic Solutions": "Cryptography protects data confidentiality, integrity, and authenticity. Symmetric encryption uses a single shared key (e.g. AES, 3DES), while asymmetric encryption uses a public/private key pair (e.g. RSA, ECC). Hashing produces a fixed-length digest used for integrity checking — common algorithms include SHA-256 and MD5 (though MD5 is considered weak). Salting adds random data to passwords before hashing to defend against rainbow table attacks. Public Key Infrastructure (PKI) binds public keys to identities through digital certificates issued by a Certificate Authority (CA). Obfuscation techniques such as steganography hide data within other files.",
  "Change Management": "Change management processes ensure that modifications to systems, software, and configurations are planned, tested, approved, and documented before implementation. A formal change request and review by a Change Advisory Board (CAB) helps prevent unauthorised or poorly tested changes from introducing security vulnerabilities. Rollback plans document how to revert to the previous state if a change causes problems. Version control systems track changes to code and configurations over time. Documenting all changes supports auditability and forensic investigations. Technical implications of changes — such as new attack surfaces or dependency conflicts — must be assessed before approval.",
  "Threat Actors": "Threat actors are individuals or groups that pose a security risk to an organisation. Unskilled attackers (script kiddies) use pre-made tools without deep technical knowledge. Hacktivists are motivated by political or social causes. Organised crime groups are primarily financially motivated. Nation-state actors have the greatest resources, sophisticated custom tools, and typically target government or critical infrastructure. Insider threats come from within the organisation — either malicious or unintentional. Shadow IT refers to unauthorised systems or software used within an organisation without IT approval. The attack surface is the total set of points where an attacker could attempt to gain access.",
  "Physical Security": "Physical security controls protect facilities, equipment, and personnel from physical threats. Fencing and bollards deter and delay unauthorised access, with bollards specifically preventing vehicle-based attacks. CCTV surveillance systems are detective controls that record activity for later review. Access control vestibules (mantraps) use two sequential locked doors to prevent tailgating — the entry of an unauthorised person following an authorised one. Door locks and access badges control entry; badge cloning attacks copy RFID/NFC credentials and are mitigated by stronger authentication such as smartcards or MFA. Brute force physical attacks attempt to force entry.",
  "Social Engineering": "Social engineering manipulates people rather than exploiting technical vulnerabilities. Phishing attacks use deceptive emails to trick recipients; spear phishing is targeted at specific individuals, while whaling targets high-profile executives. Vishing uses voice calls and smishing uses SMS. Pretexting involves creating a fabricated scenario to extract information. Motivational triggers exploited by social engineers include authority, urgency, scarcity, familiarity, social proof, and fear. Watering hole attacks compromise websites frequently visited by a target group. Influence campaigns use disinformation to manipulate public opinion at scale. Employee training and simulated phishing campaigns are primary mitigations.",
  "Malware": "Malware (malicious software) encompasses many threat types. Viruses attach to legitimate files and spread when those files are executed. Worms self-replicate and spread across networks without user interaction. Trojans disguise themselves as legitimate software. Ransomware encrypts victim files and demands payment for decryption keys. Rootkits provide hidden, persistent administrative access. Botnets turn infected machines (zombies) into a network controlled by a command-and-control (C2) server. Logic bombs trigger on specific conditions such as a date or event. Keyloggers capture keystrokes to steal credentials. Polymorphic malware changes its signature to evade detection.",
  "Vulnerabilities and Attacks": "Application vulnerabilities are flaws that attackers exploit to compromise systems. SQL injection inserts malicious SQL into database-driven applications. Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) injects scripts into web pages viewed by other users. Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF) tricks authenticated users into performing unintended actions. Buffer overflows occur when insufficient bounds checking allows data to overwrite adjacent memory. Zero-day vulnerabilities are unknown to the vendor with no available patch. Race conditions arise when security depends on the timing of events that an attacker can manipulate. Hardware vulnerabilities include firmware weaknesses, and Bluetooth vulnerabilities include Bluesnarfing (data theft) and Bluejacking (unsolicited messages).",
  "Malicious Activity": "Active attacks target running systems with intent to disrupt, steal, or manipulate. DDoS (Distributed Denial of Service) attacks overwhelm a target with traffic from many sources. DNS poisoning corrupts cached DNS records to redirect users to malicious sites. Directory traversal exploits path manipulation (e.g. `../`) to access files outside the intended directory. Replay attacks capture and retransmit valid authentication data. Session hijacking steals or forges session tokens to impersonate authenticated users. On-path (man-in-the-middle) attacks intercept communications between two parties. Indicators of Compromise (IoCs) — such as unusual outbound traffic, unknown processes, or unexpected file changes — signal a possible breach.",
  "Hardening": "System hardening reduces the attack surface by removing unnecessary features and enforcing secure configurations. Changing default credentials on all devices is a fundamental first step. Disabling unnecessary services and restricting application installations reduces potential entry points. Patch management ensures that known vulnerabilities are remediated promptly. Secure baselines define the minimum acceptable security configuration for a system type. Group Policy in Windows environments centrally enforces security settings across many machines. SELinux provides Mandatory Access Control (MAC) on Linux, limiting what even privileged processes can do. Data encryption at rest protects sensitive information stored on devices.",
  "Data Protection": "Data protection encompasses classifying, controlling, and securing data throughout its lifecycle. Data classifications range from Public to Confidential/Top Secret, with the data owner responsible for assigning the appropriate level. Data exists in three states: at rest (stored), in transit (transmitted), and in use (being processed in memory). Data Loss Prevention (DLP) tools detect and block unauthorised transfer of sensitive data. Tokenisation replaces sensitive data with non-sensitive placeholders. Data sovereignty means data is subject to the laws of the jurisdiction where it is stored. The data custodian is responsible for implementing technical controls, while the data owner sets policy.",
  "Cyber Resilience and Redundancy": "Resilience ensures systems can withstand and recover from failures or attacks. RAID (Redundant Array of Independent Disks) protects against disk failure: RAID 1 mirrors data, RAID 5 uses striping with parity. Backup strategies include full (all data), incremental (changed since last backup of any type), and differential (changed since last full backup). Recovery Time Objective (RTO) is the maximum acceptable downtime; Recovery Point Objective (RPO) is the maximum acceptable data loss measured in time. Redundant sites — hot (immediately operational), warm (partially configured), and cold (empty facility) — support business continuity. Power redundancy using UPS and generators protects against outages.",
  "Security Architecture": "Security architecture defines how systems, networks, and controls are structured to achieve security objectives. Cloud models (IaaS, PaaS, SaaS) share security responsibility between the provider and customer, with IaaS placing the most responsibility on the customer. Containerisation packages applications with their dependencies in isolated units. Infrastructure as Code (IaC) manages infrastructure through configuration files, enabling consistent and auditable deployments. Microservices isolate application components to limit the blast radius of a breach. Serverless computing shifts infrastructure management to the provider but introduces function-level attack surfaces. ICS/SCADA systems control industrial processes and often run legacy OS that are difficult to patch.",
  "Security Infrastructure": "Network infrastructure security controls filter and inspect traffic to prevent and detect attacks. Firewalls operate at various layers: stateless packet filters inspect headers; stateful firewalls track connection state; next-generation firewalls (NGFW) add application awareness. IDS (Intrusion Detection System) alerts on suspicious traffic; IPS (Intrusion Prevention System) also blocks it. The DMZ (demilitarized zone) hosts publicly accessible servers, isolated from the internal network. Web Application Firewalls (WAFs) specifically protect against application-layer attacks such as SQL injection and XSS. Network Access Control (NAC) enforces endpoint compliance before granting network access. Port 443 (HTTPS), 22 (SSH), 25 (SMTP), 53 (DNS), and 3389 (RDP) are critical ports to know.",
  "Asset and Change Management": "Asset management maintains an inventory of all hardware, software, and data assets so they can be properly secured. Mobile device deployments follow models including BYOD (Bring Your Own Device), COPE (Corporate-Owned, Personally Enabled), CYOD (Choose Your Own Device), and COBO (Corporate-Owned, Business Only). MDM (Mobile Device Management) platforms centralise control of mobile devices. End-of-life (EOL) systems no longer receive vendor patches and represent significant risk. Secure disposal requires degaussing or physical destruction of storage media to prevent data recovery. Change management (OBJ 1.3) ensures modifications go through a formal request, approval, and documentation process before implementation.",
  "Identity and Access Management": "IAM ensures the right people access the right resources. Authentication factors fall into categories: something you know (password), something you have (smart card, TOTP token), and something you are (biometrics). Multi-factor Authentication (MFA) combines two or more different categories. Single Sign-On (SSO) allows users to authenticate once for multiple systems. Federation extends SSO across organisational boundaries using trust relationships. Privileged Access Management (PAM) controls and audits administrator accounts. Access control models include RBAC (Role-Based), MAC (Mandatory — enforced by the OS), DAC (Discretionary — owner-controlled), and ABAC (Attribute-Based). Password attacks include brute force, dictionary, credential stuffing, and spraying.",
  "Security Techniques": "Security techniques are the operational controls applied to computing resources. Wireless security has evolved from WEP (broken) through WPA2 to WPA3, which uses SAE (Simultaneous Authentication of Equals) to resist offline dictionary attacks. WPA2-Enterprise uses 802.1X with a RADIUS server. Application security practices include static and dynamic code analysis, fuzzing, and secure coding standards. Email security is enforced through SPF (authorised sending servers), DKIM (cryptographic signatures), and DMARC (policy for handling failures). Endpoint Detection and Response (EDR) solutions continuously monitor endpoint activity and enable rapid response. DNS filtering blocks access to known malicious domains.",
  "Vulnerability Management": "Vulnerability management is a continuous process of identifying, assessing, prioritising, and remediating security weaknesses. Vulnerability scanners use both credentialed (deeper, agent-authenticated) and uncredentialed (external view) approaches. CVSS (Common Vulnerability Scoring System) provides a standardised severity score. CVE (Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures) provides a public identifier for known vulnerabilities. False positives report non-existent vulnerabilities; false negatives miss real ones. When a patch is unavailable, compensating controls reduce exposure. Responsible disclosure means privately reporting to the vendor before public release. Penetration testing actively exploits vulnerabilities to confirm impact, unlike scanning which only identifies them.",
  "Alerting and Monitoring": "Security monitoring collects and analyses data to detect threats in real time. SIEM (Security Information and Event Management) aggregates logs from across the environment and correlates events against rules to generate alerts. SNMP (Simple Network Management Protocol) monitors network device health and performance. SCAP (Security Content Automation Protocol) standardises the expression of security configurations and vulnerabilities. NetFlow analysis examines traffic patterns without capturing packet content, revealing anomalies such as large data exfiltration. A single pane of glass consolidates data from multiple security tools into a unified dashboard, reducing alert fatigue and improving situational awareness.",
  "Incident Response": "Incident response (IR) follows a defined process: Preparation → Identification → Containment → Eradication → Recovery → Lessons Learned. Preparation involves building IR plans, playbooks, and training teams. Identification determines whether an event is an actual incident. Containment stops the spread; eradication removes the threat; recovery restores systems. Digital forensics follows chain-of-custody procedures — evidence must be preserved through a forensic disk image before analysis. Threat hunting proactively searches for threats that have evaded automated detection. Root cause analysis (RCA) identifies the underlying cause to prevent recurrence. Lessons learned improve future response capability.",
  "Investigating an Incident": "Investigations rely on multiple data sources to reconstruct events. Packet captures (using tools like Wireshark) reveal full network-layer detail. Firewall logs show permitted and denied traffic at the perimeter. IDS/IPS logs record detected attack patterns. Authentication and OS security logs reveal account activity and privilege use. Application logs track application-level events and errors. Endpoint logs and EDR telemetry provide host-level visibility. NetFlow data shows traffic patterns without full packet content. Metadata — such as file timestamps, author fields, and GPS coordinates in images — can be critical evidence. Volatility analysis examines RAM content that would be lost on shutdown.",
  "Automation and Orchestration": "Security automation removes manual effort from repetitive tasks, improving speed and consistency. SOAR (Security Orchestration, Automation, and Response) platforms integrate security tools and automate workflows such as alert triage and incident response. APIs allow different systems to exchange data programmatically, enabling integrations between security tools. Runbooks document standard response procedures that can be executed manually or by automated systems. Automating user onboarding and offboarding ensures accounts are provisioned and deprovisioned consistently. Automating vulnerability scanning and patching reduces the window of exposure. While automation reduces human error in routine tasks, poor workflow design can itself introduce risk.",
  "Risk Management": "Risk management identifies, analyses, and responds to potential threats to organisational assets. Risk is calculated as Likelihood × Impact. Qualitative analysis uses subjective ratings (High/Medium/Low); quantitative analysis uses monetary values such as Annual Loss Expectancy (ALE = SLE × ARO). A risk register documents identified risks, likelihood, impact, owner, and mitigation status. Risk responses include: avoidance (eliminate the risk), mitigation (reduce likelihood or impact), transference (shift risk to a third party, e.g. insurance), and acceptance (acknowledge and tolerate the risk). Risk tolerance defines the maximum acceptable level of risk. Ongoing monitoring ensures the risk landscape remains current.",
  "Third-party Vendor Risks": "Third-party vendors extend the organisation's attack surface — a breach of a vendor can compromise the organisation's data or systems. Supply chain attacks target vendors or software suppliers to reach end customers (e.g. the SolarWinds attack). Vendor assessment evaluates a supplier's security posture before onboarding through questionnaires, audits, or certifications (e.g. SOC 2, ISO 27001). Vendor monitoring continues after onboarding. Contracts should include security obligations, right-to-audit clauses, SLAs, and data handling requirements. A Business Associate Agreement (BAA) is required under HIPAA when sharing Protected Health Information (PHI) with third parties.",
  "Governance and Compliance": "Governance provides the strategic framework of policies, standards, procedures, and guidelines that direct security activity. Policies are high-level management statements of intent. Standards define specific mandatory requirements. Procedures describe step-by-step processes for performing tasks. Guidelines are recommended (non-mandatory) best practices. Governance structures include boards, committees, and CISOs. Key regulations include GDPR (EU personal data), HIPAA (US health data), PCI DSS (payment card data), and SOX (US financial reporting). Non-compliance can result in fines, legal action, and reputational damage. Governance considerations span geography, industry, and data type.",
  "Audits and Assessments": "Audits evaluate whether security controls meet established policies and regulations. Internal audits are performed by the organisation's own team; external audits by independent third parties. Penetration testing actively exploits vulnerabilities — black box (no prior knowledge), grey box (partial knowledge), and white box (full knowledge) variants exist. Passive reconnaissance gathers intelligence without interacting with the target; active reconnaissance directly probes systems. Attestation of findings is formal sign-off that assessment results are accurate. Bug bounty programmes incentivise external researchers to responsibly disclose vulnerabilities. Responsible disclosure gives vendors time to patch before public release.",
  "Security Awareness": "Security awareness programmes educate employees — the human element — about their role in protecting the organisation. Training covers recognising phishing, social engineering, safe password practices, and reporting suspicious activity. Simulated phishing campaigns measure susceptibility and provide targeted follow-up training. Insider threat recognition helps employees identify warning signs in colleagues' behaviour. Remote and hybrid workers face heightened risks from home networks, personal devices, and physical eavesdropping. Creating a culture of security ensures employees view security as a shared responsibility rather than an obstacle. Policy acknowledgement and role-specific training ensure awareness is relevant and documented.",
}

